Position Overview
The Department of Physics & Astronomy at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in high-energy nuclear theory. We are particularly interested in candidates addressing fundamental questions about strongly interacting matter, from the emergence of collective behavior to the short-distance structure and dynamics of dense QCD matter. Areas of interest include QCD at finite temperature and density, systems near critical points and far from equilibrium, and probes of QCD matter across multiple scales. Approaches may include effective field theory and perturbative QCD, relativistic hydrodynamics and transport, lattice field theory, and quantum simulations.
The successful candidate will join a vibrant high-energy nuclear physics community at Vanderbilt, including a strong experimental program, offering significant opportunities for intellectual exchange across nuclear physics and related fields.
Responsibilities
• Establish and lead an independent, externally funded research program;
• Contribute to undergraduate and graduate teaching in physics;
Support the Arts & Science Core curriculum;
• Mentor graduate students and postdoctoral scholars;
• Engage with Vanderbilt’s interdisciplinary research community and external partners, including Oak Ridge National Laboratory.
